		<outline text="BioDiesl Cont...">
			<outline text="the real problem with biodiesel and ethanol">
				<outline text="Sam Abuelsamid 	

sam@abuelsamid.com &lt;mailto:sam@abuelsamid.com&gt; to adam
	 More options	  1:56 am (14 hours ago)

www.theolympian.com—article &lt;http://www.theolympian.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20050718/NEWS05/507180316/1019&gt;



Adam


The problem with these bio-fuels is that if you factor in the energy
input in the entire production process,  it takes considerably more
fossil fuel energy to produce the fuels than you get out of them.  This
factors in the fuel used by tractors an other equipment and also the
refining process itself.


&quot;But researchers at Cornell University and the University of
California-Berkeley say it takes 29 percent more fossil energy to turn
corn into ethanol than the amount of fuel the process produces. For
switch grass, a warm weather perennial grass found in the Great Plains
and eastern North America United States, it takes 45 percent more energy
and for wood, 57percent.


It takes 27 percent more energy to turn soybeans into biodiesel fuel and
more than double the energy produced is needed to do the same to
sunflower plants, the study found.&quot;


The real answer is to use less energy.


sam"/>
				</outline>
