			<outline text="Peak Oil">
				<outline text=" Stephan Segraves 	

s@badice.com &lt;mailto:s@badice.com&gt; to adam
	 More options	  Aug 28 (2 days ago)
Adam,


I finally caught up on my listening after a move. The comments by Darren
while true have a few things left out. Peak oil is a theory that relies
on the assumption that technology will not increase over time. We have
come very close to using up known oil resources in the past then a new
technology comes along and allows to continue to pumping from wells that
would not produce without the technology.


People need to understand that oil does not sit in pools underground. It
is found in porous rock. The more porous a rock the more oil it holds.
The more permeable a rock is the easier the oil flows out of it. With a
lot of the new technology we are able to go back to old wells that may
have been less permeable and pull out more oil. These technologies
include steam flooding, where the well is pumped full of steam and the
change in pressure brings the oil and steam substance to the surface.


Yes, oil is a finite resource but it is not a predictable finite
resource. Five years ago geologists were predicting 35 years of oil left
on the face of the Earth, now we are predicting 75-80 yrs. before
hitting peak oil. I am all for the use of bio-diesel but it is kind of
expensive to produce. There are other technologies as well, such as coal
to liquids, which allows us to create gasoline from coal. This is being
started in China,

www.upi.com—view.cfm &lt;http://www.upi.com/view.cfm?StoryID=19062002-041015-4320r&gt; and is pretty
exciting. Coal is great because there is a huge amount of it and with
new drilling laws we can keep


The ultimate answer though is to move away from non-renewable energy.
The oil companies are scrambling to jump on these things and the company
that I worked for (which will remain nameless, though I am sure you can
do a search and find it) has started real life experimentation with
Hydrogen in Washington D.C.


Keep up the great work Adam!


--
Stephan Segraves"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
